51
00:21:58.070 --> 00:22:17.189
Dima Gusakov | Lido: pretty vulnerable, valid… initial validator deposit process that we're gonna talk about in a second when, scoping EIPs. But you all know that, the rule for the validator data to be set is that the first deposit wins. So, whatever deposit is the first in the

52
00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:28.529
Dima Gusakov | Lido: in the queue of the pending deposits, sets the validator data, namely withdrawal credentials and stuff like that, once and for all. It cannot be changed later, and any further deposits

53
00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:39.230
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Within this deposit, everything but the validator public key and the deposit amount is ignored, so all of the other deposits are considered so-called top-ups.

54
00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:50.530
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And for some reason, well, maybe it was even sensible at the moment, the decision was made that this specific one-time builder onboarding

55
00:22:50.560 --> 00:23:00.000
Dima Gusakov | Lido: would work in a similar way, though the outcome here is a little bit different. By similar way, I mean that if you have

56
00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:07.270
Dima Gusakov | Lido: a single deposit in the deposit queue at the time of Glamsterdam Hard Fork.

57
00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:26.790
Dima Gusakov | Lido: with withdrawal credentials with the prefix of OXOB, which probably stands for builder, then any other deposits that are also in the deposit queue at the time of the hard fork will be considered top-ups, but top-ups not for the validator, but for the builder.

58
00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:36.909
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And the core of this issue is that it changes the way how this race, works. Because previously.

59
00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:53.009
Dima Gusakov | Lido: there were two possible outcomes. So, if you have a deposit within deposit, within pending deposits, you can be 100% certain that, your deposit will end up being Validator.

60
00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:55.880
Dima Gusakov | Lido: But,

61
00:23:56.020 --> 00:24:19.449
Dima Gusakov | Lido: yeah, end up being validator. And the only thing that might be different is the withdrawal credentials. So if you lose the race to an attacker, then you get a validator with invalid withdrawal credentials. And upon this assumption, protocols like Lido, and I believe many other protocols, have built their own protection systems, so that if you… if you can prove that

62
00:24:19.450 --> 00:24:36.370
Dima Gusakov | Lido: after making deposit, this initial, say, 1 ETH pre-deposit, your validator appears on the consensus layer with correct withdrawal credentials, then you're good to go, and deposits can continue. If your validator appears with wrong withdrawal credentials, but with the same public key.

63
00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:43.950
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Then, the other option for you is to prove that the deposit was invalid and discard it.

64
00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:53.160
Dima Gusakov | Lido: The problem with this builder onboarding process is that as the result of this builder onboarding process, if you have any deposits which were

65
00:24:53.160 --> 00:25:10.279
Dima Gusakov | Lido: made with validator prefix, not the builder prefix, but they stand in the deposit queue after the deposit that is made for the builder, the final outcome will be that no validator would appear at all.

66
00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:21.179
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Which means that, current protection systems on the application layer, for this very thin, like, the single moment of a GlamsterDump hard fork.

67
00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:37.589
Dima Gusakov | Lido: might find themselves in the situation where they made a deposit, expecting one of the outcomes, like, expecting a definite outcome that the validator would appear with either correct or wrong withdrawal credentials. They now have the third outcome that they were not prepared for.

68
00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:51.359
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Which is, no validator has appeared at all. And instead, the builder has appeared, and now their deposit is stolen by Builder. So, what we think from the.

69
00:25:51.390 --> 00:26:00.229
Dima Gusakov | Lido: like, from the application layer perspective and fairness, to the application layer. Would it be fair to do is, instead of treating all of this

70
00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:21.740
Dima Gusakov | Lido: further deposits with a validator prefix within the withdrawal credentials, instead of crediting those deposits to the builder, credit them to the validator. Because as far as I remember, after Amsterdam Hard Fork, you… what you can do is you can have the builder and the validator with the same public key, so no problem here at all.

71
00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:29.369
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And the only thing we are proposing to change here is to keep the outcome the same. So if you ever made a deposit.

72
00:26:29.370 --> 00:26:54.299
Dima Gusakov | Lido: With validator withdrawal credentials prefix, you should expect and you should get a validator at the end of the day and not end up in a situation where you end up with nothing at all. And I hear the comments that, okay, you can rebuild your proof system and expect this third outcome, but this sounds a little bit ridiculous to me from two points of view. The first one is

73
00:26:54.620 --> 00:27:14.369
Dima Gusakov | Lido: building anything on the application layer, auditing and stuff like that for just a one-time thing looks like a little bit of an overkill. And the second thing that the fix is actually very simple, and it would not affect builder experience at all, because if you want to top up your builder later on, you can still do it with a legit

74
00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:21.440
Dima Gusakov | Lido: deposit with the same prefix. So, why, why should we

75
00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:35.569
Dima Gusakov | Lido: mishmash or intermix both deposits to validators and builders at this very particular moment, given the fact that after that, you would still be able to have a builder and validator with the same public key.

76
00:27:35.570 --> 00:27:54.080
Dima Gusakov | Lido: So, our proposal here is to fix this small issue, and the fix is also linked to… the fix to the spec is also linked to the issue. It's a very small and pretty simple fix that would make life way easier for the majority of the application layer protocols who rely on this.

