Dima Gusakov | Lido: Which means that the same problem is solved, like, multiple, multiple, multiple, multiple times on application layer. And yes, you might say that, well, this is a problem of application layer, but at the end of the day, what Ethereum is without its application layer.

692
01:41:50.210 --> 01:41:56.480
Dima Gusakov | Lido: So what's the solution? The solution is very simple. Like, the validate… there should be a way to…

693
01:41:56.990 --> 01:42:05.890
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Agree beforehand that the validator, this validator withdrawals should go to this address.

694
01:42:06.130 --> 01:42:30.949
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And how it's achieved within this EIP? Pretty simple, in three steps. First of all, you sign the commitment where you say that this public key should have this withdrawal credentials. No race with money at stake, like it is now, when you need to actually make a deposit and then hope that everything goes well, or… and you might lose this money. No, you are not putting any money at stake here, you just sign the commitment, you submit it using a system contract on execution layer.

695
01:42:30.950 --> 01:42:37.780
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Same way as 70027251 or any other EIP that is using system contract right now, and we have plenty of them.

696
01:42:37.780 --> 01:42:52.060
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And then, when you make a deposit, you can prove via 4788 that the preregistration exists, and you have a guarantee that whenever you make a deposit, your validator would appear with correct withdrawal credentials.

697
01:42:52.480 --> 01:43:10.760
Dima Gusakov | Lido: Perfect. What's from the implementation side? And even though you might think, oh, this problem has been with us for so long, so the solution must be super complicated, it is not. On execution layer, we just need a pre-registration request contract. Again, very similar to what we already have for 70027251 and others.

698
01:43:10.770 --> 01:43:31.290
Dima Gusakov | Lido: And the code is ready, for sure, and the Lido is happy to pay for the audit if it's necessary. On the consensus layer, like, four basic operations, add pre-registrations to the Beacon state, add request processing, remove expired pre-registration so that the state would not bloat, and add extra check on deposit processing as… and that is it. That simple.

699
